<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Numa Pompilius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">trad. r. 715–673 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Numa Pompilius was, according to Roman tradition, the second king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, a Sabine from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cures">Cures</a> chosen after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a>. Legend credits him with founding Rome's religious institutions, including the priesthoods, the Vestal Virgins and the religious calendar. His reign was remembered as peaceful, though his historical existence cannot be confirmed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/numa-pompilius.jpg" alt="Numa Pompilius" width="320" height="301" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Numapisocng6371obverse.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
